  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\st0081_399734ec0e3f.png\" style=\"width: 251px; height: 250px;\" /></p><p>Given the circle has centre&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and it passes through the point&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>.</mo></math></p><p>So, the radius of the circle is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msqrt><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt></math></p><p>The equation of the line which passes through the point&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#38;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>y</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfrac><mfenced><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>x</mi></math></p><p>So, the slope of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>From the diagram, the line&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi></math>&nbsp;is perpendicular to the line&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p><p>So, the slope of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mi>Q</mi></math>&nbsp;is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>So,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>Using symmetric from of line</p><p>The coordinate of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt><mo>&#183;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt><mfenced><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mfenced></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>The coordinate of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt><mi>cos</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt><mi>sin</mi><mi>&#952;</mi></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt><mo>&#183;</mo><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mrow></mfenced><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>-</mo><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt><mfenced><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>13</mn></msqrt></mfrac></mfenced></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfenced><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfenced></math></p>"
